response:
 Let $A(a,0)$ be a focus of a parabola $y^2=4ax$ and $P, Q$ be the intersection points between an arbitrary line passing through focus and the parabola. Let $R'$ be the reflection of $Q$ on the axis of symmetry $x$ of the parabola towards the focus $A$. And let $Q', P'$ be the points which is symmetric to the points $P, Q$ about $x$-axis (in other words $|AQ'|=|PQ|$ and $|AQ'|=|PP'|$ )</think> 
<answer> Let us be given that to the parabola $y^2=4ax$. Let abs. value of the slope of the line through the focus be $b$. Thus the equation of the line will  be $y=b(x-a)$. <br/>Solving this line equation with the equation of the parabola gives us $b^2x^2+(4a-b^2)x+a^2=0$ which gives the $x$ coordinates of P and Q. The product of roots of a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ is ${c\over a}$, where $a$,$b$ and $c$ are the coefficients of the equation (see <a>here</a> from wiki
 ). Thus, the product of $x$ coordinates of P and Q are ${a^2\over b^2}$ and $x_P\cdot x_Q = {a^2\over b^2}$.</answer>

